# playwright/.env.example
#
# Copy to playwright/.env and fill in for a local run. CI gets its values
# from GitHub Actions repository variables + secrets — see
# .github/workflows/e2e.yml.
#
# THE ENV GUARD IN global-setup.ts WILL ABORT THE RUN IF ANY OF THESE
# POINT AT PRODUCTION. The guard checks at least:
#
#   * SANITY_DATASET !== 'production'
#   * DYNAMODB_ENDPOINT starts with http://localhost (or LocalStack hostname)
#   * EMAIL_BACKEND === 'file'
#   * PAYFAST_SANDBOX === 'true'
#   * ALLOW_REAL_AWS is unset
#
# This is belt-and-braces on top of backend/src/dynamo.ts's existing
# prod-write refusal.

# --- Sanity (dedicated test ACCOUNT; NEVER the prod project ID) ---
#
# The recommended pattern is a wholly separate Sanity account (e.g.
# registered with a `<you>+sanity-e2e@…` address), not just a separate
# project under your production login. Stronger isolation: a compromised
# production-account credential can't expose this dataset, and a CI
# service that holds this token can't reach production.
SANITY_PROJECT_ID=
SANITY_DATASET=test-e2e
# Editor token on the test project, scoped to the test-e2e dataset.
SANITY_API_TOKEN=
# Any 32-byte hex string. Tests use it to sign simulated webhook posts.
SANITY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- DynamoDB (LocalStack only) ---
ORDERS_TABLE_NAME=meryl-green-designs-orders
DYNAMODB_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4566

# --- Email (file backend; tests read backend/.dev-emails/) ---
FROM_EMAIL=orders@e2e.local
OWNER_EMAIL=owner@e2e.local

# --- PayFast (public sandbox merchant) ---
# Defaults in playwright.config.ts match these public test values, but the
# guard will reject anything else.
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_ID=10004002
PAYFAST_MERCHANT_KEY=q1cd2rdny4a53
PAYFAST_PASSPHRASE=payfast

# --- Admin token (any high-entropy string; constant per-run) ---
ADMIN_API_TOKEN=

# --- Ports (override if the defaults clash with another local dev session) ---
# E2E_FRONTEND_PORT=7777
# E2E_BACKEND_PORT=3001
